1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:00,486
Hugo:

2
00:00:00,486 --> 00:00:01,033
Hi Chantal.

3
00:00:01,033 --> 00:00:03,200
What did you think of the talk, then?

4
00:00:03,200 --> 00:00:03,250
Chantal:

5
00:00:03,250 --> 00:00:04,301
Hi Hugo.

6
00:00:04,301 --> 00:00:07,970
I thought it was good once I’d moved seats.

7
00:00:07,970 --> 00:00:08,263
Hugo:

8
00:00:08,263 --> 00:00:10,900
Oh – were the people beside you chatting or something?

9
00:00:10,900 --> 00:00:11,194
Chantal:

10
00:00:11,194 --> 00:00:12,371
It wasn’t that.

11
00:00:12,371 --> 00:00:15,314
I went early so that I’d get a seat

12
00:00:15,314 --> 00:00:16,786
and not have to stand,

13
00:00:16,786 --> 00:00:20,611
but then this guy sat right in front of me and he was

14
00:00:20,611 --> 00:00:21,200
so tall!

15
00:00:21,200 --> 00:00:21,410
Hugo:

16
00:00:21,410 --> 00:00:23,930
It’s hard to see through people’s heads, isn’t it?

17
00:00:23,930 --> 00:00:24,395
Chantal:

18
00:00:24,395 --> 00:00:24,861
Impossible!

19
00:00:24,861 --> 00:00:31,377
Anyway, to answer your question, I thought it was really interesting, especially what the

20
00:00:31,377 --> 00:00:34,170
speaker said about the job market.

21
00:00:34,170 --> 00:00:34,450
Hugo:

22
00:00:34,450 --> 00:00:35,011
Me too.

23
00:00:35,011 --> 00:00:38,376
I mean we know we’re going into a really competitive field

24
00:00:38,376 --> 00:00:41,460
so it’s obvious that we may struggle to get work.

25
00:00:41,460 --> 00:00:41,800
Chantal:

26
00:00:41,800 --> 00:00:46,560
That’s right – and we know we can’t all have that ‘dream job’.

27
00:00:46,560 --> 00:00:46,875
Hugo:

28
00:00:46,875 --> 00:00:47,190
Yeah,

29
00:00:47,190 --> 00:00:51,597
but it looks like there’s a whole range of … areas of work that

30
00:00:51,597 --> 00:00:55,060
we hadn’t even thought of – like fashion journalism, for instance.

31
00:00:55,060 --> 00:00:55,439
Chantal:

32
00:00:55,439 --> 00:00:58,850
Yeah – I wasn’t expecting so many career options.

33
00:00:58,850 --> 00:00:59,194
Hugo:

34
00:00:59,194 --> 00:00:59,538
Mmm.

35
00:00:59,538 --> 00:01:02,980
Overall, she had quite a strong message, didn’t she?

36
00:01:02,980 --> 00:01:03,308
Chantal:

37
00:01:03,308 --> 00:01:03,965
She did.

38
00:01:03,965 --> 00:01:07,578
She kept saying things like ‘I know you all think this,

39
00:01:07,578 --> 00:01:11,190
but …’ and then she’d tell us how it really is.

40
00:01:11,190 --> 00:01:11,499
Hugo:

41
00:01:11,499 --> 00:01:14,900
Perhaps she thinks students are a bit narrow-minded about the industry.

42
00:01:14,900 --> 00:01:15,199
Chantal:

43
00:01:15,199 --> 00:01:16,990
It was a bit harsh, though!

44
00:01:16,990 --> 00:01:19,080
We know it’s a tough industry.

45
00:01:19,080 --> 00:01:19,339
Hugo:

46
00:01:19,339 --> 00:01:21,674
Yeah – and we’re only first years, after all.

47
00:01:21,674 --> 00:01:23,490
We’ve got a lot to learn.

48
00:01:23,490 --> 00:01:24,011
Chantal:

49
00:01:24,011 --> 00:01:24,532
Exactly.

50
00:01:24,532 --> 00:01:30,260
Do you think our secondary-school education should have been more career-focused?

51
00:01:30,260 --> 00:01:30,553
Hugo:

52
00:01:30,553 --> 00:01:32,608
Well, we had numerous talks on careers,

53
00:01:32,608 --> 00:01:33,488
which was good,

54
00:01:33,488 --> 00:01:35,542
but none of them were very inspiring.

55
00:01:35,542 --> 00:01:39,650
They could have asked more people like today’s speaker to talk to us.

56
00:01:39,650 --> 00:01:40,038
Chantal:

57
00:01:40,038 --> 00:01:40,814
I agree.

58
00:01:40,814 --> 00:01:46,247
We were told about lots of different careers – just when we needed to be,

59
00:01:46,247 --> 00:01:49,740
but not by the experts who really know stuff.

60
00:01:49,740 --> 00:01:49,986
Hugo:

61
00:01:49,986 --> 00:01:53,917
So did today’s talk influence your thoughts on what career you’d like to take

62
00:01:53,917 --> 00:01:54,900
up in the future?

63
00:01:54,900 --> 00:01:55,168
Chantal:

64
00:01:55,168 --> 00:01:58,110
Well, I promised myself that I’d go through this course

65
00:01:58,110 --> 00:02:00,250
and keep an open mind till the end.

66
00:02:00,250 --> 00:02:00,486
Hugo:

67
00:02:00,486 --> 00:02:03,796
But I think it’s better to pick an area of the industry now

68
00:02:03,796 --> 00:02:06,160
and then aim to get better and better at it.

69
00:02:06,160 --> 00:02:06,475
Chantal:

70
00:02:06,475 --> 00:02:10,260
Well, I think we’ll just have to differ on that issue!

71
00:02:10,260 --> 00:02:10,520
Hugo:

72
00:02:10,520 --> 00:02:12,077
One thing’s for certain, though.

73
00:02:12,077 --> 00:02:15,971
From what she said, we’ll be unpaid assistants in the industry for quite a

74
00:02:15,971 --> 00:02:16,490
long time.

75
00:02:16,490 --> 00:02:17,030
Chantal:

76
00:02:17,030 --> 00:02:17,570
Mmm.

77
00:02:17,570 --> 00:02:17,822
Hugo:

78
00:02:17,822 --> 00:02:19,840
I’m prepared for that, aren’t you?

79
00:02:19,840 --> 00:02:20,183
Chantal:

80
00:02:20,183 --> 00:02:23,270
Actually, I’m not going to accept that view.

81
00:02:23,270 --> 00:02:23,621
Hugo:

82
00:02:23,621 --> 00:02:23,971
Really?

83
00:02:23,971 --> 00:02:28,530
But she knows it’s the case – and everyone else says the same.

84
00:02:28,530 --> 00:02:28,750
Chantal:

85
00:02:28,750 --> 00:02:31,170
That doesn’t mean it has to be true for me.

86
00:02:31,170 --> 00:02:31,679
Hugo:

87
00:02:31,679 --> 00:02:32,188
OK.

88
00:02:32,188 --> 00:02:35,240
Well – I hope you’re right!

89
00:02:35,240 --> 00:02:35,571
Chantal:

90
00:02:35,571 --> 00:02:39,540
I thought the speaker’s account of her first job was fascinating.

91
00:02:39,540 --> 00:02:39,826
Hugo:

92
00:02:39,826 --> 00:02:43,538
Yeah – she admitted she was lucky to get work being a personal dresser

93
00:02:43,538 --> 00:02:44,394
for a musician.

94
00:02:44,394 --> 00:02:48,678
She didn’t even apply for the job and there she was getting paid to

95
00:02:48,678 --> 00:02:49,820
choose all his clothes.

96
00:02:49,820 --> 00:02:50,229
Chantal:

97
00:02:50,229 --> 00:02:55,547
It must have felt amazing – though she said all she was looking for

98
00:02:55,547 --> 00:02:58,410
back then was experience, not financial reward.

99
00:02:58,410 --> 00:02:58,730
Hugo:

100
00:02:58,730 --> 00:02:59,051
Mmm.

101
00:02:59,051 --> 00:03:03,538
And then he was so mean, telling her she was more interested in her

102
00:03:03,538 --> 00:03:04,820
own appearance than his!

103
00:03:04,820 --> 00:03:05,181
Chantal:

104
00:03:05,181 --> 00:03:08,435
But – she did realise he was right about that,

105
00:03:08,435 --> 00:03:10,242
which really made me think.

106
00:03:10,242 --> 00:03:15,664
I’m always considering my own clothes but now I can see you should be

107
00:03:15,664 --> 00:03:17,110
focusing on your client!

108
00:03:17,110 --> 00:03:17,471
Hugo:

109
00:03:17,471 --> 00:03:19,640
She obviously regretted losing the job.

110
00:03:19,640 --> 00:03:19,993
Chantal:

111
00:03:19,993 --> 00:03:24,579
Well, as she said, she should have hidden her negative feelings about him,

112
00:03:24,579 --> 00:03:25,990
but she didn’t.

113
00:03:25,990 --> 00:03:26,278
Hugo:

114
00:03:26,278 --> 00:03:29,152
It was really brave the way she picked herself up

115
00:03:29,152 --> 00:03:30,878
and took that job in retail.

116
00:03:30,878 --> 00:03:32,890
Fancy working in a shop after that!

117
00:03:32,890 --> 00:03:33,248
Chantal:

118
00:03:33,248 --> 00:03:37,189
Yeah – well, she recommended we all do it at some point.

119
00:03:37,189 --> 00:03:43,280
I guess as a designer you’d get to find out some useful information, like how big

120
00:03:43,280 --> 00:03:45,430
or small the average shopper is.

121
00:03:45,430 --> 00:03:45,715
Hugo:

122
00:03:45,715 --> 00:03:48,569
I think that’s an issue for manufacturers, not designers.

123
00:03:48,569 --> 00:03:52,850
However, it would be useful to know if there’s a gap in the market

124
00:03:52,850 --> 00:03:56,845
– you know, an item that no one’s stocking but that consumers are looking

125
00:03:56,845 --> 00:03:57,130
for.

126
00:03:57,130 --> 00:03:57,453
Chantal:

127
00:03:57,453 --> 00:03:59,717
Yeah, people don’t give up searching.

128
00:03:59,717 --> 00:04:03,920
They also take things back to the store if they aren’t right.

129
00:04:03,920 --> 00:04:04,240
Hugo:

130
00:04:04,240 --> 00:04:04,561
Yeah.

131
00:04:04,561 --> 00:04:10,327
Imagine you worked in an expensive shop and you found out the garments sold there were being returned

132
00:04:10,327 --> 00:04:12,570
because they … fell apart in the wash!

133
00:04:12,570 --> 00:04:12,805
Chantal:

134
00:04:12,805 --> 00:04:15,390
Yeah, it would be good to know that kind of thing.
